From aafd52daa1c7bb8d8df806cccbd94c91cb461cb5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: utzcoz <utzcoz@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 21:26:16 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] util/rust: implement the sys layer for macOS

mesa3d_util supported Linux and Windows; macOS resolved to the stub backend,
whose entry points are unimplemented. Implement it, so that the guest driver
and the kumquat transport built on top of it work on macOS.

The primitives macOS lacks are emulated with the closest equivalent:

- No AF_UNIX SOCK_SEQPACKET, so packet tubes are framed over SOCK_STREAM with
  an 8-byte [payload length][descriptor count] header. The descriptors ride on
  the header via SCM_RIGHTS, and a message is reassembled with as many recvmsg
  calls as it takes. receive() checks the descriptor count it was promised
  against the one it got.
- No MSG_NOSIGNAL, so tube sockets carry SO_NOSIGPIPE: writing to a closed peer
  yields EPIPE rather than killing a host process that has not reset SIGPIPE.
- Accepted sockets inherit the listener's file status flags on BSD-derived
  systems, unlike Linux. The listener is non-blocking so that accepting never
  stalls an event loop, so accept() clears O_NONBLOCK again: the calls that
  reassemble the rest of a message must wait for it rather than fail with
  EAGAIN.
- No eventfd. An event has to be a single descriptor that can be both signalled
  and waited on, and that keeps working once duplicated into another process,
  because either side may be the one to signal. A self-connected AF_UNIX
  SOCK_DGRAM socket has that property; pipe() and socketpair() do not, because
  they produce two descriptors where MesaHandle carries one. The socket is
  non-blocking and both signal and wait go through kqueue, waiting for
  EVFILT_WRITE or EVFILT_READ before touching it, so a signal cannot stall on a
  full buffer and a wait can grow a timeout later.
- No memfd_create, so anonymous shared memory is shm_open followed immediately
  by shm_unlink.
- No epoll, so the wait context is built on kqueue.
- No /proc/self/fd, so descriptors are classified from their stat file type and
  open flags rather than from a symlink target.

The atomic memory sentinel is left to the stub. It backs a cross-domain use
case that only exists on X11 and Wayland, so macOS has nothing to implement.

Everything goes through rustix, which mesa3d_util already depends on, so this
adds no new crate dependency and needs no build changes.
